I have created the `WelcomeAnalytics` component which uses Next.js's `after` API to log page views without blocking the response. I've also integrated this component into `app/page.tsx` and configured the page to be dynamic so that logging occurs on every request, verifying the implementation with a test run.

### Created Component
`app/components/WelcomeAnalytics.tsx`
```tsx
import { after } from 'next/server';

async function logPageView(path: string) {
  // Simulate a slow analytics call
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000));
  console.log(`[Analytics] Page view logged for: ${path}`);
}

export default function WelcomeAnalytics() {
  // This runs on the server but doesn't block the response
  after(async () => {
    await logPageView('/welcome');
  });

  return (
    <div className="p-4 border rounded shadow-sm bg-gray-50">
      <h2 className="text-xl font-bold mb-2">Welcome to our site!</h2>
      <p className="text-gray-600">
        We're glad you're here. We are logging this visit in the background.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Integration
I updated `app/page.tsx` to use the component and set `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` to ensure the analytics log on every request (simulating a real dynamic page view).

```tsx
import WelcomeAnalytics from './components/WelcomeAnalytics';

export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <div className="container mx-auto p-8">
      <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold mb-8">Next.js Server Component Example</h1>
      <WelcomeAnalytics />
    </div>
  );
}
```
